# Alternative Sliding Window Solution

Instead of storing indices,

maintain a window of size **k**.

If a duplicate appears inside the window,

return true.

---

### Idea

```text
Window Size = k

↓

HashSet

↓

Add New Element

↓

Remove Old Element

↓

Continue
```

---

## Java Code

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {

    public boolean containsNearbyDuplicate(int[] nums, int k) {

        Set<Integer> window = new HashSet<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {

            if (i > k) {
                window.remove(nums[i - k - 1]);
            }

            if (!window.add(nums[i])) {
                return true;
            }

        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

---

# Which Solution Should You Use?

| Approach | Time | Space | Best Use |
|----------|------|-------|----------|
| HashMap (Last Index) | O(N) | O(N) | General solution |
| Sliding Window + HashSet | O(N) | O(k) | Window-based problems |

The HashMap solution is more intuitive for this problem.

The HashSet solution introduces the sliding-window technique used extensively in medium-level problems.

